<task id="067" project="spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>F5 — JobOwnershipFilter fails OPEN on a missing CreatedBy, the creator is never persisted, and a hard-coded test job serves fabricated status in production</title>
    <phase>5 Remediation — authorization</phase>
    <gate>blocked — requires 061. MUST precede 060 (they share the schema change).</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <status-note>
      Executed 2026-09-21. See notes/067-job-ownership.md.

      REPRODUCED verbatim (note §2): with no recorded owner a non-owner received another user's job status —
      200 OK for CreatedBy null AND empty; the retired test job served fabricated Running status. RED 3/5.
      A nuance the finding did not state: whitespace ("   ") ALREADY refused, because IsNullOrEmpty is false
      for it; only null and empty failed open. The fix uses IsNullOrWhiteSpace so one rule covers all three.

      SHIPPED: (1) JobOwnershipFilter now fails CLOSED — ownership must be positively PROVEN, with two reason
      codes (ownership_mismatch / ownership_unproven) behind ONE identical 403; (2) a SECOND fail-open the
      POML did not name, OfficeService.GetJobStatusAsync's in-memory check, closed the same way — fixing only
      the filter would have left the handler path open; (3) the creator is persisted at create time;
      (4) the Dataverse fallback maps it back, which is what makes failing closed safe rather than a total
      polling outage after a restart; (5) the production test-job backdoor DELETED.

      SCHEMA DECISION — 060 consumes this. NO schema change, NO new column, NO solution edit.
      sprk_processingjob.sprk_initiatedby (LOOKUP -> systemuser) ALREADY EXISTS (verified live via Dataverse
      MCP) and Models/ProcessingJob.cs:62 already declares it; it was simply never written. Create resolves
      caller OID -> systemuserid via the existing ICallerSystemUserResolver; the fallback read LEFT OUTER
      joins systemuser and returns azureactivedirectoryobjectid, so BOTH sides of every ownership comparison
      are the Entra OID by construction rather than by coincidence. GUIDs canonicalized per ADR-044.
      Escalation trigger 1 did NOT fire. Legacy rows (no initiator) are REFUSED.

      SEEDED BOTH DIRECTIONS: fail-open restored -> RED 2/6 (exactly the two fail-open cases); backdoor
      restored -> RED 1/7, and notably it returned 403 not 200, because the fail-closed filter already
      neutralizes it — the two fixes are independently effective, and the test binds to the DELETION because
      it asserts 404 (absent) rather than merely "not 200". Restored GREEN 11/0 incl. the SSE stream tests.

      Gates: build 0 warnings / 0 errors; ArchTests 191/191; CVE clean; publish branch 45.54 MB vs
      fresh-master 45.46 MB at the SAME commit 99cdfe2ea = +0.08 MB cumulative for the branch, of which this
      task's marginal contribution is ~0.00 (no new packages). Full suite 12,415/0/56 (total 12,471), reconciled: 12,403 after 063/064, +8 task 065, +4 mine.

      ONE CRITERION NOT MET: "task 061's guard no longer flags the job routes" is UNREACHABLE while 061 is
      deferred — RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.GovernedFiles contains no Api/Office/* entry, so nothing flags
      these routes and nothing would notice a filter being detached. Same unmet criterion as 062/063/064, for
      the same reason; it closes when 061 closes. Criterion 2 says "creator-OID field": the FIELD stores a
      systemuserid (it is a lookup); the OID is what the read returns, via the join. Stated, not glossed.
    </status-note>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Security fix plus a Dataverse schema addition. Tags include bff-api + auth + security + dataverse.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>ADR-017 violation on an authorization filter, and it introduces a Dataverse column that task 060 then depends on. Getting the column shape wrong forces rework in 060.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Introduces a schema change that 060 consumes; sequence 067 → 060 so the column lands once.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>061</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, api, auth, authorization, security, dataverse, job, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>5-8 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>`JobOwnershipFilter` skips its check entirely when `CreatedBy` is empty — which is exactly what the Dataverse fallback path produces — so after a restart any authenticated caller can poll any job. Persist the creator, make the filter fail closed, and delete the hard-coded test job.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. An authorization filter that fails open is worse than no filter, because it reads as protection.</role>

  <goal>Job status is disclosed only to the job's creator, on every code path including the Dataverse fallback, and no route serves fabricated job data in production.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Found 2026-09-21 independently by TWO Fable reviewers — the security review (finding F5) and the
      architecture review (finding 1), which reached it from the durability side. **Confirmed, not plausible.**
      See `notes/fable-review-2026-09-21.md` §3, where the two severity ratings are reconciled.

      **The fail-open.** `JobOwnershipFilter.cs:157` guards with
      `if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(jobStatus.CreatedBy) AND ...)` — so an **empty** `CreatedBy` **passes the
      check**. `OfficeService.cs:1543-1586`, the Dataverse fallback used after a restart or on a second
      instance, maps no `CreatedBy` at all, because the column set at
      `DataverseServiceClientImpl.cs:1773-1774` has no owner field and the `ProcessingJob` create at
      `OfficeService.cs:366-378` records no creator OID.

      Net effect: while the in-memory entry lives, ownership is enforced; once it is evicted — or on any
      other instance — **any authenticated caller can poll any job GUID**. ADR-017 states MUST NOT expose
      status without authorization checks.

      **The test-job backdoor.** `OfficeService.cs:1511-1541` returns a fabricated `Running` status for job id
      `00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001` to any authenticated caller, in production. Pre-existing on
      master (`:664`).

      **Sequencing.** Task 060 needs the same creator-OID column to make the durable store enforce ownership.
      This task lands the schema change; 060 consumes it. Doing them in the other order means adding the
      column twice or reworking 060.
    </background>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-017">Status MUST NOT be exposed without an authorization check. A filter that skips its check on missing data does not satisfy this.</constraint>
    <constraint source="this task">The filter MUST fail CLOSED: absent or unreadable ownership data means refuse, not allow. This is the whole point of the task.</constraint>
    <constraint source="this task">The creator OID is persisted at job CREATE time (`OfficeService.cs:366-378`), not inferred later. A job created before this change has no creator — define and test that legacy case explicitly rather than letting it fall through the open branch.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-044">Use typed `Guid` / `QueryExpression` for the new column, consistent with the surrounding Dataverse code.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Publish-size delta against a fresh master build; CVE check.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">REPRODUCE-FIRST: prove that with an empty `CreatedBy` — the Dataverse fallback state — a non-owner receives another user's job status. Record verbatim.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A creator-OID field exists on the job record and is written at create time. State the column/mechanism in the task note; task 060 depends on this decision.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`JobOwnershipFilter.cs:157` fails CLOSED: missing or empty ownership data refuses the request.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The Dataverse fallback path (`OfficeService.cs:1543-1586`) maps the creator, so ownership is enforced after a restart and on a second instance.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">LEGACY CASE: a job row created before this change (no creator recorded) is handled by an explicitly stated rule — refuse — and that rule is tested.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The hard-coded test job `00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001` (`OfficeService.cs:1511-1541`) is deleted, and a grep proves nothing depends on it.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">SEED BOTH DIRECTIONS: restore the fail-open guard, show the negative test RED; fix it, show GREEN. Record both.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The pane's own job polling and SSE stream still work for the owning user in both hosts — this is the shipped save-progress surface.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Task 061's guard no longer flags the job routes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Full BFF suite green with the count reconciled; ArchTests green; publish size and CVE reported.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: the fail-closed contract, the fallback path, the legacy-row case, the deleted test job and the seed directions. Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If adding a creator column to the job entity requires a solution change that cannot be made from this branch, STOP and escalate per CLAUDE.md §6 with the options — do NOT ship a filter that fails closed against data that is never populated, because that breaks every legitimate poll.</trigger>
    <trigger>If failing closed would break an existing operator or worker path that polls jobs without a user identity, STOP and escalate rather than re-opening the branch for it.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step>/conflict-check (bff-api hot path).</step>
    <step>Reproduce the fail-open; record verbatim.</step>
    <step>Decide and record the creator-OID mechanism — 060 depends on this choice.</step>
    <step>Persist the creator at create time; map it on the fallback path.</step>
    <step>Make the filter fail closed; define and test the legacy-row rule.</step>
    <step>Delete the test-job backdoor; grep for dependents first.</step>
    <step>Tests; seed both directions; verify the pane's polling and SSE still work.</step>
    <step>Build, full suite, ArchTests, publish size, CVE.</step>
    <step>Write `notes/067-job-ownership.md`, recording the schema decision for 060. Update TASK-INDEX.md: 067 → ✅.</step>
  </steps>

